{
  "schema": "toroid-mlt-reference",
  "version": "0.0.79",
  "what_this_is": "printed mean-length-per-turn values for iron-powder toroids, with the outside diameter / inside diameter / height those values belong to. The point of this table is NOT to supply an MLT for a core in data/powder_cores.json - it is to put a MEASURED band on how far a real winding's turn length sits from the flat rectangular-section perimeter 2*H + (OD - ID) that the geometry alone gives. Every number here is a transcription of what the page prints, page by page, with the URL beside it; nothing is computed into this file.",
  "source": {
    "vendor": "the T-type powder toroids are Micrometals cores; these pages are the German shop Amidon / Profi-Electronic reprinting each core's data sheet (German labels: Betriebstemperatur, Windungszahl ...)",
    "url_pattern": "https://www.amidon.de/contents/de/d585_0.html",
    "fetched": "2026-11-01",
    "how": "curl of the shop page, then the 'mlt - mean length per turn' line read off the stripped text: six printed quantities in the order le, Ae, Ve, WA, sa, mlt - the LAST one is the MLT"
  },
  "units": {
    "od_mm": "mm, printed outside diameter",
    "id_mm": "mm, printed inside diameter",
    "ht_mm": "mm, printed height (the shop prints inches for the imperial sizes and both are on the page; the mm column is transcribed here)",
    "mlt_mm": "mm per turn (the page prints cm; x10)",
    "al_nh_per_n2": "nH/N^2 as printed",
    "printed_le_cm": "cm",
    "printed_ae_cm2": "cm^2",
    "printed_ve_cm3": "cm^3",
    "printed_wa_cm2": "cm^2 (minimum effective window area)",
    "printed_surface_area_cm2": "cm^2 (unwound)",
    "permeability": "the page's printed reference mu_i, not a name"
  },
  "column_order": [
    "model",
    "od_mm",
    "id_mm",
    "ht_mm",
    "mlt_mm",
    "permeability",
    "al_nh_per_n2",
    "test_winding",
    "printed_le_cm",
    "printed_ae_cm2",
    "printed_ve_cm3",
    "printed_wa_cm2",
    "printed_surface_area_cm2",
    "density_g_cm3",
    "url",
    "notes"
  ],
  "why_a_band_and_not_a_formula": "the pages print the MLT but not the winding it was measured with, so there is no vendor definition to copy. Three closed forms were tried against these thirteen printed values and none is exact: the flat rectangular section 2*H + (OD - ID) comes out 20-32 % short of every printed value (printed/flat = 1.248 ... 1.461; the winding stands off the surface and the wire has its own thickness), while the two fitted forms 2*H + (OD + ID)/2 and 2*H + OD - 0.4*ID land within 7.5 % and 5.0 % respectively - but both were fitted to these same thirteen points and are nobody's definition. So this file supplies the measured RATIO band, which is a fact about real windings, and the calculator multiplies the geometric perimeter by it instead of pretending to know the formula.",
